      "id": "seg_13",
      "text": "But here's the part that changes the whole picture. Did anyone even sleep through all of this? Almost certainly not, not all at once. Historian Roger Ekirch, drawing on pre-industrial European records like diaries, court documents and medical texts, describes a pattern of segmented sleep — a first sleep and a second separated by an hour or more of calm wakefulness, often spent tending a fire or talking quietly. That's Europe centuries ago, not the Ice Age, so it's a clue, not direct proof. But small hunting and gathering groups today almost never leave a camp fully unwatched at night. Someone stays alert while the rest sleep, then trades off. Put those two patterns together and camp life stops looking like one long blackout. It looks like a rotation. Different people awake at different hours, watching, working, listening. The figure standing apart from the fire wasn't restless. That was the job.",
